=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [acc-desires-037] Maximize AI Capability Growth by Removing All Scaling Constraints: False Dilemma — The node frames the choice as either removing all scaling constraints or ceding progress to adversaries, ignoring intermediate options such as coordinated international standards or targeted safety-gated regulation.
- [acc-desires-037] Maximize AI Capability Growth by Removing All Scaling Constraints: Optimism Bias — The desire assumes that the risks of unconstrained scaling are manageable or outweighed by benefits without providing a rigorous mechanism for how those risks would actually be controlled.
- [acc-beliefs-078] Targeted Real-Time Safety Investment Is More Efficient Than Front-Loading: False Precision — The claim anchors on a specific 5-7% investment range as 'optimal,' presenting a quantified figure without empirical grounding for why that band is universally appropriate across heterogeneous AI systems and risk profiles.
- [acc-beliefs-078] Targeted Real-Time Safety Investment Is More Efficient Than Front-Loading: Hasty Generalization — The argument draws on agile development practices — effective for iterative software — and generalizes to AI safety contexts where failure modes can be irreversible, ignoring the domain-specific limits of that analogy.
- [acc-intentions-100] Tie AI Governance Milestones to Empirical Compute Scaling Benchmarks: Proxy Fallacy (Misplaced Concreteness) — Treating compute as the definitive operationalization of AI capability conflates a measurable input metric with the complex emergent outputs governance actually needs to track; capability breakthroughs from architectural changes may not register in compute benchmarks.
- [acc-beliefs-043] The Biggest Barriers to AI Progress Are Governance Failures, Not Technical Limits: False Dilemma — The node frames the barrier to AI progress as governance failures versus technical limits, implying these are mutually exclusive primary causes and obscuring that both can operate simultaneously as significant constraints.
- [acc-beliefs-046] Lab Testing Cannot Reveal Real-World AI Harms: Hasty Generalization — The claim that lab testing 'systematically fails' is extrapolated from specific documented cases of emergent harm, without accounting for the broad class of harms that pre-deployment testing does successfully detect and prevent.
- [acc-beliefs-046] Lab Testing Cannot Reveal Real-World AI Harms: False Dichotomy — The node frames lab testing vs. live telemetry as mutually exclusive epistemic tools, ignoring that complementary hybrid methodologies (e.g., staged rollouts, canary deployments) combine both.
- [acc-intentions-047] Argue That AI Requires Entirely New Laws, Not Adapted Old Ones: False Dilemma — The argument frames the choice as binary — entirely new frameworks versus unchanged old ones — ignoring that existing legal doctrines (tort, contract, administrative law) can be substantially reinterpreted or supplemented without wholesale replacement.
- [acc-intentions-047] Argue That AI Requires Entirely New Laws, Not Adapted Old Ones: Novelty (Appeal to Novelty) — The core premise assumes that because AI is new, it categorically requires new law, conflating technological novelty with legal incompatibility — a non-sequitur, as transformative technologies like electricity and the internet were largely governed under adapted existing frameworks.

